+/**
+ * Hardware pixel backend for the pushbuffer executor. See nv2a_gl.h.
+ *
+ * What is mirrored, and from where:
+ *   - the three software pixel loops in nv2a_pb_exec.c (raster_triangle,
+ *     xf_rows with and without combiners), one fragment program family each;
+ *   - nv2a_rc_eval (nv2a_combiner.c), translated register for register into
+ *     GLSL, one program per distinct combiner set-up, cached;
+ *   - put_pixel's blending, colour mask and clip rectangle as GL state;
+ *   - the host-side depth buffers (one per zeta offset, four of them).
+ * Texels are decoded on the CPU by the executor's own sample_tex and uploaded
+ * as RGBA8, so every format the software path reads looks the same here.
+ *
+ * ---- Guest memory and the GPU: who holds the truth ----------------------
+ *
+ * Every guest colour surface that is drawn into gets a GL texture + FBO,
+ * keyed by its resolved guest address. The simplest scheme that is correct
+ * for this title:
+ *
+ *   1. The GPU copy is authoritative from the moment the surface is first
+ *      used. It is created by UPLOADING what guest memory holds, so anything
+ *      the CPU put there beforehand is kept.
+ *   2. Guest memory is brought up to date ("download", glReadPixels straight
+ *      into the surface with its own pitch) only when something is about to
+ *      read it there: a frame dump, the framebuffer window at a flip, a
+ *      texture stage that reads those bytes in a way that cannot use the FBO
+ *      directly, or the surface being re-created with another shape. A
+ *      download first checks (full hash) that the CPU has not written those
+ *      bytes in the meantime; if it has, nothing is written and (4) applies.
+ *   3. A texture stage whose address, pitch and format are a surface's binds
+ *      that surface's texture directly (render to texture; the title's
+ *      blurred menu backdrop). Sampling the surface being drawn into goes
+ *      through a snapshot copy.
+ *   4. CPU writes into a surface after (1) are detected, not assumed away: a
+ *      sampled hash of the surface's guest bytes is kept from the last moment
+ *      the two copies agreed, and re-taken once per frame when the surface is
+ *      bound for drawing or sampling. If the bytes changed, the CPU wrote
+ *      them (nothing else writes there), so they are uploaded and win. This
+ *      also covers a freed render target whose memory became a texture.
+ *
+ *   5. Two surfaces of different shape at one address (the title's scratch
+ *      render targets: 160x120 and 128x128 share memory in Halo 2 and are
+ *      switched twice a frame) are two GL surfaces, keyed by address, pitch
+ *      and depth. Switching between them moves no pixels: on the console the
+ *      second would start as the first one's bytes read at another pitch,
+ *      which is noise no title can use, and here it starts as whatever it
+ *      held last. Reading guest memory takes the one drawn into last.
+ *   6. A swizzled texture stage naming a surface (render target that is a
+ *      power-of-two texture) also binds the surface's texture: on the
+ *      console both sides address the same Morton-ordered texels, so the
+ *      picture is the same as binding the linear copy.
+ *
+ * Not covered: the title reading a surface out of guest memory by itself
+ * (a screenshot, a thumbnail) sees the last download, not the current frame.
+ * RECOMP_GPU_SYNC=1 downloads the presented surface at every flip for that.
+ *
+ * Ordinary textures are cached by (address, format, size) and validated by a
+ * hash of their bytes once per frame. A texture whose bytes have changed in
+ * the last 600 frames, or that is younger than 8 frames, is "dynamic" and is
+ * also validated whenever the title touches that stage's registers, so one
+ * rewritten in place between two draws of a frame (video frames, a glyph
+ * cache) is re-uploaded. The rest are not: the title writes the stage
+ * registers before nearly every draw, and hashing each texture at each use
+ * was 9.5 MB a frame in the first campaign level.
+ */
